Description: Implements a robust Hotelling T-squared control chart for batch
    processes that combines Minimum Covariance Determinant (MCD) estimation
    per batch with Multiple Factor Analysis weighting of the batch
    covariances. The weighting reduces the influence of contaminated batches
    on the Phase I reference covariance, which keeps the chart sensitive to
    mean shifts in Phase II when the historical batches are not clean. The
    upper control limit is parametric, adjusted for the effective batch size
    left by MCD trimming; a bootstrap alternative was evaluated in the
    reference paper and discarded, so it is not implemented here. The method
    detects shifts in the process mean only: changes in the covariance
    structure are out of scope. Also provided are a classical Hotelling
    T-squared implementation as a baseline for comparison, a control chart
    that labels the out-of-control batches for reporting, a plot of the
    Phase I batch weights against the uniform weight that shows which
    calibration batches carry the most internal dispersion, and a generator
    of contaminated batch data for simulation studies. The method and its
    calibration are described in Frutos-Galarza et al. (2026); the weighting
    scheme follows Abdi, Williams and Valentin (2013)
    <doi:10.1002/wics.1246> and the robust estimator follows Rousseeuw and
    Van Driessen (1999) <doi:10.1080/00401706.1999.10485670>.
